WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:05.360
Did you know that each cell has thousands of different types of proteins?

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.750
Proteins are the building blocks of our cells,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.030
and they have many different important functions,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.350
like helping cells grow and communicate with other cells.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.730
To be able to make all of these different proteins, our cells use a set of instructions stored 

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.620
in our genes,

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.140
which are made of DNA.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.530
Knowing that our cells need to make proteins,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:28.830
it may surprise you to learn that the instructions are actually very hard to read.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:30.340
What do we mean by this?

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.940
Let's say you are making brunch for your friends,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.350
and one person asks you to scramble eggs,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.990
then add Swiss cheese,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:37.510
tomato,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.750
ham and spinach.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.200
It seems simple,

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.240
but in our cells,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.250
this sentence would look like this.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.870
The words in those instructions are listed in order,

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.600
but they're not continuous.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.590
There's a lot of gibberish in between,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.790
so the sentence doesn't make sense.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.790
But how do our cells unravel the confusing DNA instructions to make a protein?

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.140
They do this by first copying the DNA into another type of nucleic acid 

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.640
called RNA, and then cutting the gibberish regions out of the RNA. 

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.190
This process is known as RNA splicing.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.000
Let's understand what an RNA is,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.410
how the process of splicing takes place, and why the cells bother 

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:23.400
storing and then cutting out all of the gibberish. To start to learn about the 

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.510
process of splicing,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.060
let's dive down into the nucleus of the cell,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.750
which houses our DNA.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.580
DNA is first transcribed into RNA.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.130
This RNA is called a pre-mRNA because it has not yet gone through splicing,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.620
it still has the gibberish mixed in with the instructions.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.410
You'll notice that the RNA contains the information for producing a protein,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.460
which are called exons,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.050
as well as extraneous, interspersed material.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.350
These gibberish regions, which do not code for protein, are called introns.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:01.290
The exons are also called protein coding regions as they contain 

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.310
information that instructs the synthesis of the final protein,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.070
whereas the introns are a type of non protein coding region.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.880
The introns are removed by a large protein, an RNA complex called 

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.410
the spliceosome,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:20.200
here shown between the first and second exons. On either end of the intron 

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.210
are particular RNA sequences,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.370
which the spliceosome brings together.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.910
Then the sliceosome cuts the bases close to exon number one,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.470
forming a loop called a lariat.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.850
Next, the RNA gets cleaved close to exon number two.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.390
Finally,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.450
the spliceosome joins the two exons together,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.500
releasing the intron,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.660
which eventually gets degraded.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.500
This process occurs for all the introns in a given RNA.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.870
Some genes have no introns,

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:51.740
while others have hundreds.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.230
The final spliced product is a mature messenger RNA, or in the case of your 

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:57.810
brunch,

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.460
an omelet with Swiss cheese,

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:00.000
tomato,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.070
ham and spinach.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:07.020
The mature messenger RNA is now ready to be transported out of the nucleus and 

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.340
into the cytoplasm,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.690
where it can be translated into protein.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:16.480
You'll notice that there are also non protein coding regions on opposite ends of the 

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.510
mature mRNA.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.630
These are called the 5' and 3' untranslated regions, UTRs.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.780
Since these regions are not removed from the pre-mRNA during RNA splicing,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.300
they are not considered introns.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:36.290
Although they do not make proteins, these UTRs have important functions in the initiation of 

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:37.030
translation,

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.210
which is the process of making protein from RNA, and in the stabilization of 

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.250
the mRNA molecule to prevent degradation.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.660
Now that we know how splicing works,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.190
let's talk about why we even have introns and exons in the first place.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.560
One amazing feature of splicing is that cells can make multiple kinds of mRNA 

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:01.270
products from one gene by including or excluding particular exons.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.680
This is called alternative splicing.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.650
Let's go back to the brunch dish your friend requested.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.040
If all the introns are spliced out,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.110
it reads scramble eggs, then add Swiss cheese,

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:12.680
tomato,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.950
ham and spinach.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:15.960
Let's say another friend joins you,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.090
but she's a vegetarian.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.550
Alternative splicing would remove the exon corresponding to ham so she 

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.570
could have an omelet with Swiss cheese,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.650
tomato and spinach.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.600
A lot of variety can be introduced this way.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:33.700
This allows cells to create many different proteins from just a small number 

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.160
of RNA messages.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.670
Cells in different organs or cells that are at different stages of development can 

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:45.380
use alternative splicing to produce proteins suited for their unique functions.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:46.380
Overall,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.360
splicing is an extremely important process.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.350
Because of it,

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.190
we can produce the many varieties of proteins that contribute to the rich complexity 

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:55.750
of life.

